  "query_preview": ".. (Wednesday through Monday) \n\nRegardless of whether today proves to be a convective bust \nlocally, the active ring of fire pattern will remain intact \nthrough Thursday morning. Additional shortwave energy will \noccasionally ripple across central Illinois along the lingering \nbaroclinic zone. Dewpoints pooling in the mid-70s, combined with\nnighttime low-level jet (LLJ) veering over the surface \nboundary, will sustain moderate buoyancy each night. \nIntermittent overlaps of 25-35 kt deep-layer shear will keep an\norganized severe weather threat in play through midweek. \n\nBy Thursday evening, a more defined, broad upper trough axis \nwill dig across the eastern Great Lakes, driving the surface \nboundary south through our CWA as a cold front. This will flush \naway the oppressive heat and high humidity, if only temporarily.\nNBM guidance reflects afternoon highs dropping into the lower \n80s by Friday. \n\nThis initial cooldown looks brief, as the frontal boundary \nwavers back northward as a warm front Friday night into \nSaturday. Showers and thunderstorms will likely accompany the \nwarm-frontal passage Saturday, with additional rain chances \narriving Sunday into Monday as a potent shortwave trough digs \nacross the Plains and drives a stronger cold front through the \nregion. \n\nDespite nuance differences among medium-range guidance \nregarding exact frontal timing over the weekend, models exhibit \nstrong consensus that a deeper continental polar (cP) air mass \nwill sweep into the Midwest by Monday and Tuesday, delivering a \nmuch-needed, extended break from the active convective regime.",
